#!/bin/sh
# A stand-in `claude` for register item 871's gate: a pane whose ARGV names a
# conversation, which is the only thing that gives `Pane::agent_session` a value.
#
# ## What it has to be, and what it does not
#
# `hooks::launched_identity` reads the conversation out of the ARGV — the
# program's basename picks the agent and `--session-id <uuid>` names the
# conversation — so this file's CONTENTS are nothing to the claim. Its NAME is
# everything: called anything else, the daemon reads no identity and the run
# records no conversation, which is the state item 871 was opened in.
#
# So it only has to keep its pane alive while a run is submitted and its row is
# read. `exec cat` does that, exactly as this suite's other long-lived panes do.
#
# ## Why it is a tracked file rather than one the gate writes
#
# Register item 467, `ssh` beside it verbatim: a file any process holds open for
# writing cannot be executed, and this harness runs its cases on THREADS of one
# process, so a sibling forking to spawn a program inherits the write handle
# until its own exec. Item 465 measured that shape at 10 failures in 30 runs.
# The gate links this file into the case's own directory instead.
#
# It needs no `tail.sh`: unlike `ssh`, nothing here varies per case.
stty -echo 2>/dev/null
exec cat
